al HTML will be around 30KB, which is manageable. For the JSON output, I'm going to use single quotes for HTML attributes to minimize escaping, and I'll convert all the dialogue quotes to typographic curly quotes along with smart apostrophes — this eliminates the need to escape double quotes in the JSON while also improving the typography. The JSON-LD structured data will still need escaped quotes, but that's contained and manageable.\n\nI'm keeping the HTML as one continuous line to avoid newline issues, and the CSS will be compact with a green and gold color scheme, a sticky header, and styled buttons. Now I'm ready to transcribe the full Chapter 2 text accurately with the curly quotes applied, maintaining proper paragraph breaks according to Project Gutenberg standards.\n\nFor navigation, chapters beyond 3 will link to the book purchase page. I'm also finalizing the key improvements: SEO optimization with metadata and structured data, mobile-responsive typography, a persistent sticky header with conversion CTAs, a hero section with social proof and clear call-to-action buttons, a reorganized table of contents in a scannable grid format that marks free chapters and funnels readers toward the offer, an email capture form to build the mailing list, and product cards displaying different editions with anchored pricing for direct sales.\n\nI'm also adding a mid-content audiobook CTA banner, fixing the typo \"The Cyclon\" to \"The Cyclone\" with proper typography, and including footer sharing links for viral distribution. Now I'm writing the HTML with all these elements carefully structured.\n\nWriting the HTML... Writing page styles... Still writing styles... Adding the hero section with the book title, author, ratings, and call-to-action buttons, then structuring a table of contents with 24 chapters where the first few are free to read and the rest link to the purchase page with a locked icon indicator. The #get-the-book section needs an email form at the top for the