ategy: I'll add SEO fundamentals like proper title tags and schema markup for the book, improve readability with typography choices like serif fonts and line-height, and create a sticky navigation header. The main conversion points are affiliate links for the hardcover and audiobook, plus an email signup offering free coloring pages or a reading guide. I'll place CTAs both mid-content and at the end, and add prev/next navigation between book chapters to keep readers engaged across the series.\n\nI'm also planning support buttons for donations, ad slots ready for AdSense, social sharing buttons, and a reading progress bar with back-to-top functionality using minimal JavaScript. The site needs to be fully responsive for mobile. Now I'm ready to write the HTML structure, making sure to preserve the chapter text exactly as it appears, and I'll organize it with the sticky header containing the site title and book navigation links. I'll wrap up the remaining chapters with a comment placeholder since I'm only fully transcribing the first three, then add the closing elements: a purchase CTA block, previous/next navigation, footer with public domain attribution, and JSON-LD schema markup. I'm also planning some lightweight JavaScript for a progress bar, back-to-top button, and smooth scrolling, plus I'll use an Amazon affiliate link with a placeholder tag that I can note for future updates. Now I'm ready to start transcribing the actual chapter text. Writing the JSON structure... I'm laying out the key improvements for monetization and user experience: SEO metadata to drive organic traffic, responsive typography that keeps readers engaged, a hero section with multiple purchase options, strategically placed affiliate modules throughout the content, an email capture to build a subscriber list, and navigation between books.\n\nI'm also adding engagement features like a reading progress bar, social sharing buttons, and non-intrusive ad slots for display revenue, while preserving