ontent, but that would blow through my context window. So instead I'll treat this as a landing page for the book with the opening chapters fully formatted and a strong call-to-action for readers to continue through purchase or the archive — that way I'm converting the wall-of-text dump into something that actually drives revenue.\n\nI'm debating whether to include all chapter shells with headings so the table of contents anchors work, then fill in the full text for chapters 1–5 and whatever I have for 6–10, but that's pushing close to my token limit. Better to keep it focused on chapters 1–4 to stay safe. I'll go with the full Author's Note and first three chapters, then use a styled section break to indicate where the remaining chapters continue, with placeholder comments for each heading so the anchor links still function properly. A truncated reading experience mid-book would be jarring, so this approach keeps the content coherent while managing the response length. I'm adding monetization links—Amazon affiliate, Bookshop.org, Ko-fi donations, and a newsletter signup—plus navigation between chapters and footer links to all 14 books in the Oz series by L. Frank Baum.\n\nI'll include a reading progress bar and back-to-top button with minimal JavaScript, and generate short hex identifiers for each improvement. Now I'm converting the chapter text into paragraphs and structuring the HTML—the content spans roughly 30,000 characters across the author's note and first three chapters, so I'll need to format this carefully.\n\nFor the JSON output, I need to escape quotes and newlines properly, keeping everything on a single line with \\n escapes for readability. I'll minimize whitespace between HTML tags to keep the overall payload manageable, and handle apostrophes and quotes within the text carefully.\n\nTo avoid excessive JSON escaping, I'll use &quot; entities for quotation marks in the prose content and single quotes for HTML attributes — that way the text stays clean