m weighing whether to include all 26 chapters or just the first few as a preview. Including chapters 1–9 would be around 90k characters, which risks being too large for the context window. I think the best approach is to keep chapter 1 fully displayed as a sample and note that the complete text is preserved in the structure, even if I can't include every chapter due to space constraints. I'm hitting token constraints with the full text, so I need to be strategic about what to include. Chapters 1–3 as a free preview makes sense, with a clear call-to-action for the complete book since this is public domain material. That way I deliver real value without exceeding output limits.\n\nFor the page structure, I'm building a clean, SEO-optimized layout with proper metadata, a sticky navigation header with the site branding and purchase buttons, and a hero section that establishes the book's position in the Oz series. The typography will be serif-based and readable, with a narrow column width to keep line length comfortable.\n\nThe content strategy is to offer the first three chapters in full to hook readers, then use a conversion block to guide them toward either continuing online, downloading the free ebook from Project Gutenberg, purchasing a physical edition through affiliate links, or signing up for a newsletter. For chapters 4 through 26, I need to decide whether to show full text, teasers with links, or just headings—showing just teasers with dead links would be frustrating, so I should either include the full text or make the links actually functional. I'll make the table of contents entries for chapters 4–26 link to a \"Continue Reading\" section instead of dead anchors, and point readers to either that section or directly to Project Gutenberg for the full text. I should also add a footer with navigation to related Oz books, starting with the series order.\n\nWriting the HTML now... Writing the HTML page... Finishing up the improvements list with sticky navigation, 