to outsiders, but assured Woot that the Tin Woodman was kind, honest, and true—qualities that made his subjects forget he was different from ordinary folk. When Woot expressed a desire to meet him, the servant went inside to ask permission, finding the Tin Woodman and the Scarecrow delighted at the prospect of entertaining a visitor. They welcomed the boy immediately, and as Woot made his way through the ornate hallways of the castle, he marveled at the grandeur surrounding him. The Scarecrow questions why Woot would leave such a comfortable home, and Woot explains that he found his former life too dull and predictable—he wanted to explore other parts of Oz to encounter new people and experiences, which is what drove him to become a wanderer nearly a year ago. The Wanderer seizes the opportunity to ask for food, and the Tin Woodman promptly summons a servant to bring a generous meal on tin dishes and furniture, all polished to a mirror shine. The boy asks him to share his story, and the Tin Woodman settles into his throne to explain how he came to be made of tin, beginning with his past as a woodchopper in the Munchkin Country. I continued bringing her firewood, and we fell in love, but when the Witch of the East discovered our plans to marry, she grew furious at losing her slave. I foolishly defied her, and in revenge, she enchanted my axe so it severed my right leg as I worked in the forest. aring she was proud of me. The Witch saw the kiss, and was more angry than before. When I went to work in the forest next day, my axe, still being enchanted, slipped and cut off my other leg. Again I hopped—on my tin leg—to my friend the tinsmith, who kindly made me another tin leg and fastened it to my body. So I returned joyfully to Nimmie Amee, who was much pleased with my glittering legs and promised that when we were wed she would always keep them oiled and polished. But the Witch was more furious than ever, and as soon as I raised my axe to chop, it twisted around and cu