e Historical Document · Free Download</span><h1>The Wizard of Oz: <span>A Musical Comedy</span><br>The Original 1902 Stage Script</h1><p class='sub'>Decades before Judy Garland clicked her heels, L. Frank Baum brought Oz to the stage in a spectacular musical extravaganza that took Chicago and Broadway by storm. Read the complete original script — all three acts, songs and stage directions included.</p><div class='ctas'><a class='btn' href='OzPlay.pdf' download>Download the Complete Script — Free PDF</a><a class='btn ghost' href='#preview'>Read a Preview First</a></div><p class='trust'>106 pages · All three acts · Instant download · No signup required</p></section><section><div class='container'><h2>Why This Script Matters</h2><div class='cards'><div class='card'><h3>A Broadway Sensation</h3><p>Adapted by L. Frank Baum from his own beloved 1900 novel, this musical extravaganza premiered in 1902 and became one of the biggest stage hits of its era — the show that first proved Oz could conquer any medium.</p></div><div class='card'><h3>An Oz You&#39;ve Never Seen</h3><p>Meet characters cut from the famous film: Cynthia the &#8220;lady lunatic&#8221; searching for her enchanted sweetheart Niccolo Chopper, the deposed King Pastoria, the poet Dashemoff, and the good witch Locusta of the North.</p></div><div class='card'><h3>Songs Lost to Time</h3><p>The script preserves the show&#39;s comic numbers — including &#8220;Niccolo&#39;s Piccolo,&#8221; &#8220;In Michigan,&#8221; and &#8220;Carrie Barry&#8221; — complete with lyrics, whistling choruses, and original handwritten stage notes.</p></div></div></div></section><section><div class='container'><h2>What&#39;s Inside the Script</h2><div class='toc'><ul><li><strong>Act I</strong> — The Kansas prairie farm, the cyclone, and the descent of the house into the Munchkin Country; the road through the forest; and the deadly Poppy Field.</li><li><strong>Act II</strong> — The Emerald City and the court of the Wizard, with the Scarecrow, the Tin Woodman, and a kingdom in comic upheaval.</li><li><strong>Act III</strong> — The edge of the domain of Oz, Pastoria&#39;s revenge, the headsman&#39;s block, and Locusta&#39;s spectacular final enchantment.</li></ul></div></div></section><section class='preview' id='preview'><div class='container'><h2>Preview: The Curtain Rises</h2><div class='script'><h3>&#8212; ACT I &#8212;</h3><p class='scene'>Scene II: The Munchkin Country — Land of Oz. The house descends, crushing the Wicked Witch. Discovered: Chorus of Munchkins.</p><p class='who'>1st Girl</p><p>Here&#39;s a catastrophe!</p><p class='who'>2nd Girl</p><p>What a dreadful storm.</p><p class='who'>3rd Girl</p><p>Never in the land of Oz has there been one like it.</p><p class='who'>4th Girl</p><p>I wonder from what unknown country the storm blew this house.</p><p class='who'>5th Girl</p><p>Such a strange dwelling — so different from ours.</p><p class='dir'>(Noise off stage, shouts, cries, etc. Enter Reuben and Simon with Cynthia, whose costume and make-up suggest madness.)</p><p class='who'>Reuben</p><p>Here&#39;s an enchantress. Do any of you know her?</p><p class='who'>All</p><p>No!</p><p class='who'>Girl</p><p>Who are you, and where do you come from?</p><p class='who'>Cynthia</p><p>My name is Cynthia, and I am a lady lunatic.</p><p class='dir'>(All recoil from her)</p><p class='who'>2nd Girl</p><p>Does the asylum know you are out?</p><p class='who'>Cynthia</p><p>Fear nothing. I am not dangerous.</p><p class='who'>1st Girl</p><p>She&#39;s a sorceress. To the river with her.</p><p class='who'>Cynthia</p><p>That&#39;s no way to treat a perfect lady lunatic.</p><p class='who'>2nd Girl</p><p>If you sink we&#39;ll believe you.</p><p class='who'>Cynthia</p><p>But I will not sink. I&#39;ve been taking swimming lessons from a banker.</p><p class='who'>1st Girl</p><p>Swimming lessons from a banker?</p><p class='who'>Cynthia</p><p>Yes — he taught me how to float a loan.</p><div class='fade'></div></div><div class='cta-row'><a class='btn' href='OzPlay.pdf' download>Keep Reading — Get the Full Script (Free PDF)</a></div></div></section><section class='signup'><div class='container'><h2>Join the Oz Collectors&#39; Circle</h2><p>Get rare Oz scripts, photographs, sheet music, and newly digitized treasures from the golden age of Oz delivered to your inbox — free.</p><form action='/subscribe' method='post'><input type='email' name='email' placeholder='Your email address' required aria-label='Email address'><button type='submit'>Send Me Oz Treasures</button></form><p class='privacy'>No spam, ever. Unsubscribe anytime.</p></div></section><section class='finale'><div class='container'><h2>Step Behind the Curtain of 1902</h2><p style='max-width:560px;margin:0 auto 28px'>The complete script — every scene, song, and stage direction from the show that made Oz a legend — is yours to read right now.</p><a class='btn' href='OzPlay.pdf' download>Download the Complete Script — Free</a><p style='margin-top:18px'><a href='https://themarvelouslandofoz.com/'>Explore more Oz history at The Marvelous Land of Oz &rarr;</a></p></div></section></main><footer><p>The Wizard of Oz: A Musical Comedy (1902) by L. Frank Baum is in the public domain.</p><p style='margin-top:8px'><a href='https://themarvelouslandofoz.com/'>Home</a> · <a href='OzPlay.pdf'>Original PDF</a></p></footer></body></html>\", \"improvements\": [{\"What\": \"Converted the raw PDF into a full HTML landing page with a styled hero, headline, and value proposition for the 1902 script.\", \"Why\": \"A bare PDF link has no persuasion layer, no branding, and no calls to action; an HTML landing page lets visitors understand the value before committing, dramatically increasing engagement and downstream conversions.\", \"Hash\": \"a1f3c9\"}, {\"What\": \"Added multiple prominent CTAs ('Download the Complete Script — Free PDF') in a sticky header, hero, mid-page, and final section, all linking to OzPlay.pdf.\", \"Why\": \"Repeated, high-contrast calls to action at natural decision points capture intent wherever the reader is on the page, lifting click-through and download rates.\", \"Hash\": \"b7d201\"}, {\"What\": \"Included an authentic in-page script preview of Act I with a fade-out effect leading into a 'Keep Reading' CTA.\", \"Why\": \"Content teasers with a curiosity gap are a proven conversion pattern: readers invested in the excerpt are far more likely to download the full document.\", \"Hash\": \"c4e88d\"}, {\"What\": \"Added an email capture section ('Join the Oz Collectors' Circle') offering rare Oz materials.\", \"Why\": \"Email capture converts one-time visitors into a recurring audience that can be monetized through future offers, merchandise, and content — the primary revenue lever for a niche fan site.\", \"Hash\": \"d95f42\"}, {\"What\": \"Added 'Why This Script Matters' benefit cards and a 'What's Inside' table of contents summarizing all three acts, unique characters, and lost songs.\", \"Why\": \"Clearly articulating unique value (characters and songs not in the movie) builds desire and justifies the download, while scannable structure keeps low-attention visitors engaged.\", \"Hash\": \"e60b7a\"}, {\"What\": \"Added SEO metadata: title, meta description, canonical URL, Open Graph tags, and schema.org CreativeWork JSON-LD marking the PDF as a free encoding.\", \"Why\": \"PDFs rank poorly and share badly; rich metadata improves search visibility and social sharing previews, increasing qualified organic traffic and thus total conversions.\", \"Hash\": \"f17c3e\"}, {\"What\": \"Added trust and friction-reduction signals ('106 pages · Instant download · No signup required', public-domain notice, no-spam promise) and links back to the site homepage.\", \"Why\": \"Reducing perceived risk and effort increases conversion, and homepage links keep visitors in the site funnel instead of dead-ending at a file.\", \"Hash\": \"0a92b5\"}, {\"What\": \"Implemented a responsive, mobile-friendly layout with legible serif typography styled to match the vintage Oz theme.\", \"Why\": \"A large share of traffic is mobile; a PDF is painful to read on phones, so a responsive page prevents bounces and preserves conversions from mobile visitors.\", \"Hash\": \"1b64d8\"}]}","refusal":null,"reasoning":"I'll fetch the URL to see what content is there.I need to decode the base64-encoded PDF and extract its text content using a PDF parsing library.The URL points to a PDF of the 1902 Wizard of Oz musical script, but I need to create an improved HTML page instead — converting this content into a well-designed web page with conversion elements like book purchase links, site exploration options, and newsletter signups. Let me check the main site to understand what they're selling and how to best integrate this content.I can't fetch the homepage, so I'll need to work directly with the PDF content. The full script is around 88KB of text, which is substantial. I'm weighing whether to include the entire play in the HTML response—it would be feasible but potentially very large, possibly exceeding reasonable output limits. I'm considering whether a well-designed HTML page with the complete text is the best approach or if I should explore an alternative.\n\nA better strategy is to create a conversion-optimized landing page for \"The Wizard of Oz: A Musical Comedy (1902)\" that features the beginning of the script with proper structure, plus CTAs and related elements, rather than embedding all 88K characters. Including the full text would likely push the response to 40-60K tokens, which is too risky. Instead, I'll build a landing page with a hero section, title, description, and a call-to-action.I'll create a landing page that showcases the historic 1902 script with an attractive presentation, includes a preview of Act I to provide value and help with SEO, and features strong calls-to-action for downloading the full PDF, joining a mailing list, and exploring the broader collection. I'll 