n issue is handling all the dialogue quotes throughout the chapters—they'll need to be escaped as \\\" in the JSON. I'm considering using single-quoted HTML attributes to minimize escaping overhead, since the prose text contains extensive quoted dialogue that would otherwise require careful escaping. The entire HTML needs to be on a single line as valid JSON, so I'll write it with spaces instead of newlines to avoid manual escape sequences. For HTML attributes I'll use single quotes to avoid escaping, and in JavaScript and CSS I'll stick with single quotes too. JSON-LD would require escaping a lot of double quotes, so I'm going to skip it and just use meta and Open Graph tags instead — simpler and still good for SEO. Now I need to convert the dialogue quotes in the prose to curly quotes.\n\nI'll write a Python script that takes the author's note and both chapters, converts them to HTML paragraphs with smart curly quotes applied, then outputs the complete HTML as a single line with single-quoted attributes so I can embed it directly into my answer. Adding the Librivox link as another resource option, setting up a simple newsletter signup form with a thank-you message, and linking to adjacent chapters plus the index before I generate the script.I'm thinking through how to efficiently generate the full HTML page as a single Python string without re-printing large chunks, since whatever gets printed enters the context and I can copy it. The key is to compose everything in Python first, then print it once so I can include it in my final answer.I need to fix the reference to note_html_sig and remove the double quotes from the onclick handler. Instead of using JavaScript for smooth scrolling, I'll rely on the CSS scroll-behavior property that's already set, then add an id='top' anchor to the body or progress div so the href='#top' link works without any quotes or special characters.\n\nFor the signature, I'm extracting the last two paragraphs from the original note HTML an