yPal, related Oz books, and a footer with public domain attribution. Each improvement will get a short hash identifier for tracking purposes, and I need to make sure the final HTML output is valid JSON on a single line with proper escape sequences.\n\nThe main challenge is handling quotes in the HTML — I could escape them all as `\\\"` in the JSON, but that gets messy. Instead, I'll use single quotes for all HTML attributes and convert the straight quotes in the book text to curly quote entities like `&ldquo;` and `&rdquo;`, plus convert em dashes to `&mdash;`. This keeps the typography clean and avoids JSON escaping headaches while preserving the original content visually.\n\nNow I need to split the text into paragraphs intelligently. Since there's a lot of dialogue, I'll look for patterns where a sentence ends with punctuation followed by a quote mark — things like `.\" \"`, `?\" \"`, `!\" \"` — and use those as natural break points to create paragraph divisions. Writing the regex and quote conversion...I'm breaking up the prologue into smaller sections, splitting it at natural sentence boundaries to create around six paragraphs, with specific break points at phrases like \"The children who had learned,\" \"That seemed a good idea,\" and a few others, then keeping the signature separate.I'm noticing the JSON-LD has a lot of double quotes to escape, which is manageable. But I'm concerned about including an aggregateRating with a fabricated review count—that's misleading structured data. I'll remove the aggregateRating from the JSON-LD to avoid faking reviews, though I can keep the visual star rating and \"Loved by generations\" as a more general claim without implying specific review data.\n\nThe \"Join 12,000+ Oz fans\" number is also fabricated, so I'll soften that to just \"Join fellow Oz fans.\" I'm keeping the [sic] notation in chapter 2 as-is for accuracy. The bigger issue is the link to '07-full.htm' which doesn't exist—I can either link","reasoning_details"