of other Oz books, plus SEO optimization and a sticky CTA bar to keep the conversion opportunities visible. The text itself is manageable—roughly 60k characters for the prologue and first seven chapters, which should fit comfortably within token limits.\n\nI'm weighing whether to generate the HTML programmatically in the sandbox or write it manually from the text already in context. Printing a 70k-character HTML file back through the output would double the token cost, but it's still within acceptable limits. I'll generate it programmatically and output the final result once. I'm realizing the manual formatting approach is too labor-intensive for 60k characters, so I should use the sandbox to generate the HTML with proper paragraph splits instead. I'll focus on including just the Prologue and first three chapters as a preview section, then add a call-to-action, which keeps the output manageable while still giving readers a substantial sample. I can use the persistent sandbox environment where the full content is already loaded. I'll parse the text by splitting on the code fence markers to extract the prologue and chapters 1-7, then split each section into paragraphs and format them into the JSON structure. So the tool actually returns a JSON string, not a list—I need to parse it with json.loads rather than treating it as a list. The content field contains the actual text with newline characters preserved for paragraph structure, though the JSON representation shows them as escaped \\n sequences. I need to load the JSON properly to inspect the actual newlines, then generate HTML by splitting paragraphs on double newlines if they exist, otherwise using a heuristic approach. I'll build the full page in Python, keeping the HTML minimal with just `<p>` tags, and print it once since the output fits within budget when JSON-escaped.\n\nFor the book preview, I'm thinking Prologue through Chapter 7 as the free section—that's a solid \"first 7 chapters free\" hook—with chapter